window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-25"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "There are two different questions about one grid here, so I want to separate the routing parts from the staging part early.", "questions": ["Does the traversal cost include the origin and the destination cells?", "Is the move set arbitrary, so I should treat east, south and south-east uniformly rather than special-casing two of them?", "When two routes tie on cost, which one should the planner emit?"], "assumptions": ["Traversal cost counts every cell on the route, origin and destination included.", "Ties are resolved by entering each cell by the earliest move in the order east, south, south-east.", "An empty floor plan, a ragged one, a negative cost, an empty move set, and an unreachable destination are all typed failures."], "edgeCases": ["a closed aisle in the first row or first column, which cuts off everything after it", "a closed origin or destination", "a one-cell floor, which is a legal route of one position", "a floor whose destination is reachable by a diagonal chassis and not by a rail one"], "restate": "Count monotone routes across a floor with closed aisles, find the cheapest crossing under a configurable move set, emit that route, and size the largest all-open square."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One grid, two different meanings for a table entry. Parts 1 to 3 fold over the cells a route could have arrived from; Part 4 folds over three neighbours to grow a region. Naming the entry before writing the transition is the whole job.", "dataStructures": ["A table of Int for the route count and a table of optional Int for the cost, where nil is unreachable and cannot win a minimum the way a large sentinel can.", "One private predecessors helper defines the movement model once, so the counting fold and the cost fold cannot disagree about what a legal step is."], "types": ["Aisle is an enum with an associated cost rather than a matrix of Ints with a magic blocked value, so a closed aisle cannot be confused with a cheap one.", "GridPosition is a small Hashable struct, which compares cleanly in expectations where a tuple would not."], "composition": "cheapestCost and cheapestRoute share one private costTable: the cost is its corner and the route is that table walked backwards. Part 1 does not reuse the cost table, because a count and a minimum are different folds, but it does share the predecessors helper. largestClearSquareSide and clearSquareCount are the maximum and the sum of one more table.", "tradeoff": "The cost table could collapse to a single row, which would halve the memory and forfeit the route entirely. Part 3 is the reason to keep the whole table.", "trap": "Representing unreachable with a very large number. Adding a traversal cost to it makes it finite, and it starts winning minima on exactly the floors where the answer should be a refusal."}, "coding": {"order": ["Validate the floor plan first, in one private helper every part calls: empty, ragged, and negative cost.", "Write the predecessors helper next, driven by the move set, and make Part 1 the first caller.", "Build the cost table, return its corner for Part 2, then walk it backwards for Part 3.", "Finish with the square table and read the maximum and the sum off it."], "commentary": ["An entry here counts the routes that end on this cell, so a closed aisle contributes nothing and that is what propagates along the first row.", "The move set is a parameter, so this same loop plans for a rail chassis and a diagonal one.", "Unreachable is nil rather than a large number, so it cannot win a minimum after a cost is added to it.", "For the route I ask which arrival cell explains this entry exactly, which is why I walk backwards rather than choosing the cheaper neighbour forwards.", "Part 4 keeps the grid and the loop and changes only what an entry means, which is the point of the part."], "checkIn": "Before Part 4, say: the next table is on the same grid but an entry means something else. Do you want me to state the new meaning before I write it?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the all-open three by three floor: the first row and column are all ones and the corner comes out at six.", "Walk the worked floor with the middle aisle closed: the counts finish at two, and the cost table fills 1, 4, 5 across the top, 2 and unreachable and 6 down the middle, and 6, 8, 7 along the bottom.", "Walk the route back from the corner of that floor: 7 minus 1 is 6, which only the cell above explains, and the walk ends up along the first row and down the last column for five cells.", "Walk the square table on the same floor: no two by two square survives the closed aisle, so the side is one and the count is the eight open cells."], "tests": "Run swift test --filter Problem25WarehouseGridRoutePlannerTests and read the four suites in part order; the greedy-first-step floor and the diagonal floor are the two that separate a real table from a plausible guess.", "complexity": "Every part is O(rows times columns) time, times the size of the move set for the folds that use it. Space is one table, which the reconstruction in Part 3 requires; the count and cost tables alone would collapse to a single row.", "moreTime": "The same table shape covers a floor whose rows differ in length once ragged input is accepted rather than refused, and a move set of south and south-east turns it into a triangle. The genuinely different variant is one where the objective depends on the future rather than the past, which has to be filled from the destination backwards; that is the clearest demonstration that iteration order follows the dependencies and not habit."}, "code": "/// One cell of the warehouse floor: either drivable at a known cost, or closed\n/// for restocking.\npublic enum Aisle: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case open(traversalCost: Int)\n    case blocked\n}\n\npublic struct GridPosition: Hashable, Sendable {\n    public let row: Int\n    public let column: Int\n\n    public init(row: Int, column: Int) {\n        self.row = row\n        self.column = column\n    }\n}\n\n/// The forward moves a chassis can make. A rail chassis has east and south; a\n/// free chassis adds southEast. Every move increases the row or the column, so\n/// no route can revisit a cell.\npublic enum DriveMove: Hashable, Sendable, CaseIterable {\n    case east\n    case south\n    case southEast\n}\n\npublic enum RouteError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case emptyGrid\n    case raggedGrid\n    case negativeTraversalCost\n    case emptyMoveSet\n    case unreachableDestination\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct RoutePlanner: Sendable {\n    /// The order ties are resolved in when a route is reconstructed. It is the\n    /// declaration order of DriveMove, stated once so the tie rule is a fact\n    /// about the planner rather than an accident of a loop.\n    private static let moveOrder: [DriveMove] = [.east, .south, .southEast]\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Count monotone routes\n\n    /// How many east/south routes cross the floor from the north-west corner to\n    /// the south-east one.\n    ///\n    /// One entry counts the routes that end on that cell, so a cell's entry is\n    /// the sum of the entries of the cells a picker could have arrived from. A\n    /// blocked cell ends no routes and therefore contributes nothing, which is\n    /// what propagates a closed aisle along the first row and the first column.\n    public func routeCount(grid: [[Aisle]]) throws(RouteError) -> Int {\n        try validate(grid)\n        let railMoves: Set<DriveMove> = [.east, .south]\n        var routes = Array(repeating: Array(repeating: 0, count: grid[0].count), count: grid.count)\n\n        for row in 0..<grid.count {\n            for column in 0..<grid[row].count {\n                guard case .open = grid[row][column] else { continue }\n                if row == 0 && column == 0 {\n                    routes[0][0] = 1\n                    continue\n                }\n                let position = GridPosition(row: row, column: column)\n                for predecessor in predecessors(of: position, in: grid, moves: railMoves) {\n                    routes[row][column] += routes[predecessor.row][predecessor.column]\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        return routes[grid.count - 1][grid[0].count - 1]\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Cheapest traversal cost\n\n    public func cheapestCost(grid: [[Aisle]], moves: Set<DriveMove>) throws(RouteError) -> Int {\n        let costs = try costTable(grid, moves)\n        guard let destination = costs[grid.count - 1][grid[0].count - 1] else {\n            throw .unreachableDestination\n        }\n        return destination\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Emit the cheapest route\n\n    /// The route is read back out of the same table the cost came from. A cell is\n    /// on the cheapest route when its own cost plus a predecessor's cost equals\n    /// its entry; ties resolve to the earliest move in the planner's move order.\n    public func cheapestRoute(grid: [[Aisle]], moves: Set<DriveMove>) throws(RouteError) -> [GridPosition] {\n        let costs = try costTable(grid, moves)\n        var position = GridPosition(row: grid.count - 1, column: grid[0].count - 1)\n        guard let total = costs[position.row][position.column] else { throw .unreachableDestination }\n\n        var reversedRoute = [position]\n        var remaining = total\n        while position.row != 0 || position.column != 0 {\n            let cost = traversalCost(of: position, in: grid) ?? 0\n            let step = predecessors(of: position, in: grid, moves: moves).first { predecessor in\n                costs[predecessor.row][predecessor.column].map { $0 + cost == remaining } ?? false\n            }\n            guard let step else { break }\n            reversedRoute.append(step)\n            remaining -= cost\n            position = step\n        }\n\n        return reversedRoute.reversed()\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Largest clear staging square\n\n    /// Parts 1 to 3 folded over the cells a route could arrive from. This folds\n    /// over a cell's three northern and western neighbours to grow a region, so\n    /// an entry now means the side of the largest all-open square whose\n    /// south-east corner is that cell. Same grid, same row-major order, a\n    /// different meaning for one entry.\n    public func largestClearSquareSide(grid: [[Aisle]]) throws(RouteError) -> Int {\n        try squareTable(grid).lazy.map { $0.max() ?? 0 }.max() ?? 0\n    }\n\n    /// Every entry of that table is also the number of all-open squares whose\n    /// south-east corner is that cell, so the count is the same table summed\n    /// rather than maximised. The table is the asset; the answer is a fold.\n    public func clearSquareCount(grid: [[Aisle]]) throws(RouteError) -> Int {\n        try squareTable(grid).reduce(0) { $0 + $1.reduce(0, +) }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared machinery\n\n    private func validate(_ grid: [[Aisle]]) throws(RouteError) {\n        guard let firstRow = grid.first, !firstRow.isEmpty else { throw .emptyGrid }\n        for row in grid where row.count != firstRow.count { throw .raggedGrid }\n        for row in grid {\n            for aisle in row {\n                if case .open(let cost) = aisle, cost < 0 { throw .negativeTraversalCost }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func traversalCost(of position: GridPosition, in grid: [[Aisle]]) -> Int? {\n        guard case .open(let cost) = grid[position.row][position.column] else { return nil }\n        return cost\n    }\n\n    /// The cells a picker could have arrived at `position` from, in the planner's\n    /// move order. The movement model lives here so Part 1 and Parts 2 and 3\n    /// cannot disagree about what a legal step is, even though they fold over\n    /// these cells differently.\n    private func predecessors(\n        of position: GridPosition,\n        in grid: [[Aisle]],\n        moves: Set<DriveMove>\n    ) -> [GridPosition] {\n        var found: [GridPosition] = []\n        for move in Self.moveOrder where moves.contains(move) {\n            let candidate: GridPosition\n            switch move {\n            case .east: candidate = GridPosition(row: position.row, column: position.column - 1)\n            case .south: candidate = GridPosition(row: position.row - 1, column: position.column)\n            case .southEast: candidate = GridPosition(row: position.row - 1, column: position.column - 1)\n            }\n            guard candidate.row >= 0, candidate.column >= 0 else { continue }\n            found.append(candidate)\n        }\n        return found\n    }\n\n    /// The cheapest cost of reaching each cell, or nil where no legal route\n    /// reaches it. Part 2 reads its corner; Part 3 walks it backwards.\n    private func costTable(_ grid: [[Aisle]], _ moves: Set<DriveMove>) throws(RouteError) -> [[Int?]] {\n        try validate(grid)\n        guard !moves.isEmpty else { throw .emptyMoveSet }\n\n        var costs: [[Int?]] = Array(repeating: Array(repeating: nil, count: grid[0].count), count: grid.count)\n        for row in 0..<grid.count {\n            for column in 0..<grid[row].count {\n                let position = GridPosition(row: row, column: column)\n                guard let cost = traversalCost(of: position, in: grid) else { continue }\n                if row == 0 && column == 0 {\n                    costs[0][0] = cost\n                    continue\n                }\n                let reachable = predecessors(of: position, in: grid, moves: moves).compactMap { costs[$0.row][$0.column] }\n                if let best = reachable.min() {\n                    costs[row][column] = best + cost\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return costs\n    }\n\n    /// The side of the largest all-open square ending at each cell.\n    private func squareTable(_ grid: [[Aisle]]) throws(RouteError) -> [[Int]] {\n        try validate(grid)\n        var sides = Array(repeating: Array(repeating: 0, count: grid[0].count), count: grid.count)\n        for row in 0..<grid.count {\n            for column in 0..<grid[row].count {\n                guard case .open = grid[row][column] else { continue }\n                if row == 0 || column == 0 {\n                    sides[row][column] = 1\n                } else {\n                    sides[row][column] = 1 + min(\n                        sides[row - 1][column],\n                        sides[row][column - 1],\n                        sides[row - 1][column - 1]\n                    )\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return sides\n    }\n}\n"};
